<td id="S5.T1.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.43</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">This table quantifies the magnitude of the constraint violations in end-effector space for the regenerated <em id="S5.T1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">datasets</em> at each perturbation level.
The magnitude of the constraint violation in each dataset is controlled by varying the <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> parameter in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>. The constraint violations for the policies trained on these datasets are presented in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics for Evaluation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a coarse evaluation of policy performance, rollouts of trained policies are categorized into four outcomes:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">I.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Success:</span> Successful grasp and transportation of the box with <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both</span> grippers;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">II.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-Gripper Success:</span> transportation of the box, but one of the grippers loses its grasp along the way;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">III.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Box Drop Failure:</span> Successful grasp of the box, but failure during transportation;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">IV.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Failure</span>: Failure to pick up the box.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> provides a visualization of these four categories. To produce a binary success rate, the first two outcomes are considered a “success” and the latter two a “failure.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that these are not metrics for constraint violation, but rather metrics for task completion. For instance, a policy that cannot maintain a constant grasp may still complete the task due to compliance in the grippers, but could potentially exert damaging contact forces on the object. The latter is a consequence of constraint violation, but is not captured by our metrics for task completion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Instead, we measure constraint violations during box transportation as the mean position and orientation error of the relative transform between the end-effectors. We choose to measure constraint violation in the end-effector space instead of configuration space since it is a reasonable proxy for constraint violation in the robot’s output space, it is easier to compute, and it is more interpretable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.SS1.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.01404v1/four_case_figure.png" id="S5.SS1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/125;" width="476" height="125" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.SS1.fig1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel">Key:</p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
